{"text": "Now the front of the robe turns towards you. Find the narrow strip on either side of the opening. That is the okumi. It gives each front enough width to cross the other. When the robe is worn, the wearer's left front lies over her right. Nothing here is cut to a figure. The robe is as long as she is tall, and the reach from her spine to the sleeve opening is her own. So a kimono could be taken apart. Where kimono were everyday wear, robes were unpicked and remade as seasons, uses and owners changed. A panel swapped, a piece extended, the silk washed flat and sewn up again. There is a saying for it, \"Unpick a kimono and it turns back into cloth.\" There is no crest on it. In one Tottori town in the late 1960s, crests had all but left clothes, except on ceremonial dress. The shape is shared by the most formal robe and the plainest. What sets them apart is the cloth and the season it was made for. The next room begins with the cloth.", "duration": 64.239, "timestamps": {"word": [{"word": "Now", "start": 0.14, "end": 0.36}, {"word": "the", "start": 0.42, "end": 0.5}, {"word": "front", "start": 0.54, "end": 0.74}, {"word": "of", "start": 0.78, "end": 0.84}, {"word": "the", "start": 0.88, "end": 0.96}, {"word": "robe", "start": 1.02, "end": 1.26}, {"word": "turns", "start": 1.34, "end": 1.68}, {"word": "towards", "start": 1.74, "end": 2.16}, {"word": "you.", "start": 2.26, "end": 2.42}, {"word": "Find", "start": 2.78, "end": 2.96}, {"word": "the", "start": 3, "end": 3.1}, {"word": "narrow", "start": 3.16, "end": 3.48}, {"word": "strip", "start": 3.58, "end": 3.82}, {"word": "on", "start": 3.88, "end": 4.019}, {"word": "either", "start": 4.1, "end": 4.32}, {"word": "side", "start": 4.4, "end": 4.62}, {"word": "of", "start": 4.66, "end": 4.72}, {"word": "the", "start": 4.76, "end": 4.88}, {"word": "opening.", "start": 5, "end": 5.5}, {"word": "That", "start": 6.02, "end": 6.16}, {"word": "is", "start": 6.24, "end": 6.38}, {"word": "the", "start": 6.44, "end": 6.5